{"@context":{"lean":"lean:","block":"block:","imports":{"@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"lean:module:QOU.Archimedean.IdealBoundaryGeodesicPlane","@type":"LeanModule","name":"QOU.Archimedean.IdealBoundaryGeodesicPlane","imports":["lean:module:Init","lean:module:QOU.Archimedean.GeodesicFaceH3","lean:module:Mathlib.Topology.Compactification.OnePoint.Basic"],"declarations":7,"chunks":1},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Archimedean.verticalHalfSpaceThrough_disjoint_plane","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Archimedean.verticalHalfSpaceThrough_disjoint_plane","kind":"theorem","line":113,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Archimedean/IdealBoundaryGeodesicPlane.lean#L113-L124","doc":"The oriented half-space lies on one side of its plane: on the plane the signed\narea is `0`, so the defining strict product cannot be positive.  (A sanity link\nbetween the half-space and its bounding geodesic plane, both defined from the same\ntwo vertices.) ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Archimedean.verticalHalfSpaceThrough","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Archimedean.verticalHalfSpaceThrough","kind":"def","line":103,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Archimedean/IdealBoundaryGeodesicPlane.lean#L103-L111","doc":"**The oriented vertical half-space** cut by `verticalPlaneThrough b c`, on the\nside of the far vertical line over the finite reference point `i`.  A point is in it\niff its projection lands strictly on the same side of the line `bc` as `i` does —\ndetected by the two signed areas having the same (nonzero) sign, i.e. positive\nproduct.  In an ideal tetrahedron with a face through `∞, b, c`, the interior\nreference `i` is the opposite (fourth) vertex, so this is the half-space \"toward the\ninterior\". ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Archimedean.verticalPlaneThrough","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Archimedean.verticalPlaneThrough","kind":"def","line":94,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Archimedean/IdealBoundaryGeodesicPlane.lean#L94-L101","doc":"**The vertical geodesic plane through `∞`, `b`, `c`** (`b, c` finite): the\nEuclidean vertical plane over the line through `b` and `c` in `∂H³`.  A point of\n`ℝ³` lies on it iff its horizontal projection is on that line, i.e. iff the signed\narea of `(b, c, projection)` vanishes — at ANY height, which is what \"vertical\"\nmeans.  (This is `geodesicPlaneThrough ∞ b c`; the geodesic classification above is\nthe sole cited input.) ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Archimedean.sarea_swap","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Archimedean.sarea_swap","kind":"theorem","line":88,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Archimedean/IdealBoundaryGeodesicPlane.lean#L88-L90","doc":"Swapping the two spanning points negates the signed area. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Archimedean.sarea_affine","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Archimedean.sarea_affine","kind":"theorem","line":82,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Archimedean/IdealBoundaryGeodesicPlane.lean#L82-L86","doc":"`sarea` written out as an affine functional of its last argument:\n`sarea u v w = (u.2 − v.2)·w.1 + (v.1 − u.1)·w.2 + (u.1 v.2 − u.2 v.1)`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Archimedean.sarea","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Archimedean.sarea","kind":"def","line":74,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Archimedean/IdealBoundaryGeodesicPlane.lean#L74-L80","doc":"**Twice the signed area** of the Euclidean triangle `(u, v, w)` — equivalently\nthe 2-D cross product of `v - u` and `w - u`.  It vanishes exactly when `w` lies on\nthe line through `u` and `v`, and its sign says which side of that line `w` is on.\nThis single scalar carries both the vertical-plane equation (`= 0`) and its oriented\nhalf-space (sign), so the whole vertical-face story is expressed through it. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Archimedean.BdryPoint","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Archimedean.BdryPoint","kind":"def","line":68,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Archimedean/IdealBoundaryGeodesicPlane.lean#L68-L72","doc":"**A boundary point of `H³`.**  `∂H³` is the plane `z = 0` (identified with\n`ℝ × ℝ`, i.e. `ℂ`) together with the single far point `∞` — its one-point\ncompactification.  The four ideal vertices of an ideal tetrahedron are four\n`BdryPoint`s. ","signatureChunk":0}]}